// Conformance row K21 — a NON-GENERIC method of an imported USER module embeds
// into the caller's frame with its own module's private siblings intact
//
// Designs 96/104 promise that a suspending call drives at ANY nesting depth,
// and design 150 makes user modules and std one uniform import model. Put
// together, they say this program runs: `Runner.run` lives in `embedmod`, it
// suspends, and `main` calls it — so the coroutine transform embeds `run` as a
// sub-frame of `main`'s. `run`'s body names `inner` and `boost`, which are
// private to `embedmod`.
//
// Design 210's non-generic path is what makes that true: the embed carries the
// declaration-time annotations, so the spliced body is never re-resolved in the
// entry module's namespace — where `inner` and `boost` are, correctly, not
// names at all.
//
// COMPILE-FLAGS: --module-path embedmod={TESTDIR}/modules/embedmod
// EXPECT: success
// EXPECT-OUTPUT:
// 108

import embedmod.{Runner}

func main() {
    let r = Runner(tag: 1)
    print(r.run())
}
